#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184069 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc36f534-6b27-5619-a29e-9954d0310adb" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Two-Piece Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "0ea91343-560e-5a95-a60c-089e452868b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9965" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x)$, defined below?\n${e^{x - 1},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 1, \\\\ 1 - x,\\end{vmatrix}x > 1$\n\n1. $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$\n2. $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$\n3. $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x = 1$.\n\nLet's start by computing $h(1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh(1) &= e^{1 - 1} \\\\\n&= e^{0} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n$lim_(x → 1^{-})h(x)|= e^{1 - 1}; = e^{0}; = 1; = h(1)$\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n$lim_(x → 1^{+})h(x)|= 1 - 1; = 0; \\ne h(1)$\n\nStatement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=1$, it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous at this point. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184073 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25229703-4cfb-5010-ae5c-f6a54503ced3" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Three-Piece Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "b67fa054-0258-593d-a153-10f945953cc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9972" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$, defined below?\n${2 - x,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ 2,\\end{vmatrix}x = 0; \\frac{4}{x + 2}, \\mid x > 0$\n\n1. $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x = 0$\n2. $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x = 0$\n3. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 0$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x = 0$.\n\nFirst, we note that\n\n$$\nf(0) = 2\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x = 0$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n$lim_(x → 0^{-})f(x)|= 2 - 0; = 2$\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x = 0$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n$lim_(x → 0^{+})f(x)|= \\frac{4}{0 + 2}; = \\frac{4}{2}; = 2; = f(0)$\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=0$, which implies that the function is continuous at $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
